-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **Precision:** SVQ codebooks are optimized while indices are fixed. Ensure gradient flow is maintained only through codebooks during iterations 29K–30K [S1].
-- **Boundaries:** The transition at iteration 15K must ensure that SH coefficients are fully trained up to that point to provide a meaningful DC initialization [S1].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### Appearance Embedding
-Associates each training image with a unique embedding vector to model appearance changes such as lighting and shadows [S2, unofficial]. The embedding dimension is set to 8 [S2, unofficial]. During validation, the mean of training appearance embeddings is used for all validation images to ensure consistent representation [S2, unofficial]. This variant is used in NeRF-based methods (e.g., SCALAR-NeRF) rather than Gaussian Splatting [S2, unofficial].
+### [Appearance Embedding](14693-appearance-embedding.md)
+Associates each training image with a unique learnable vector to model appearance variations such as lighting changes and shadows. It sets itself apart from standard Gaussian Splatting by decoupling appearance from the static 3D geometry, allowing the radiance field to adapt to per-image illumination conditions without altering the underlying Gaussian parameters.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** The method adds negligible overhead to the standard 3D Gaussian Splatting training loop, except for the one-time K-means clustering at iteration 29K [S1].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Introduces storage for appearance feature vectors (dimension $D=3$) per Gaussian [S1]. SVQ reduces memory footprint by storing codebooks and indices instead of full feature vectors [S1].
-- **Measured Performance:** Experiments conducted on NVIDIA RTX 4090 using the Mini-Splatting framework [S1]. Total training time is 30K iterations [S1].
-- **Strengths:** Stabilizes view-dependent rendering by delaying appearance feature training; reduces memory usage via SVQ [S1].
-- **Limitations:** Requires careful scheduling of hyperparameters (e.g., $\lambda=2$, iteration counts) [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The method adds minimal computational overhead during the first 15,000 iterations. The introduction of appearance features increases memory usage and gradient computation cost from iteration 15,000 onwards. The SVQ phase (29K–30K) involves codebook optimization but uses fixed indices, reducing the search space compared to full quantization training [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage increases at iteration 15,000 when appearance features (dimension $D=3$) are added to each Gaussian [S1].
+*   **Hardware:** Experiments were conducted on an NVIDIA RTX 4090 [S1].
+*   **Framework:** Implemented within the Mini-Splatting framework [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Improves training stability by allowing geometry to converge before introducing appearance complexity. The delayed initialization prevents early-stage instability from appearance parameters.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires a fixed total iteration count (30K) for the schedule to be effective. The simplification step at 20K is critical and sensitive to the $\lambda=2$ factor [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Originated in the context of optimized minimal 3D Gaussian Splatting implementations [S1]. The approach builds on [Spherical Harmonics Initialization](3163-spherical-harmonics-initialization.md) by leveraging trained SH coefficients for static appearance initialization [S1]. The appearance embedding variant originates from NeRF-W and SCALAR-NeRF [S2, unofficial].
+This schedule originates from the "Optimized Minimal 3D Gaussian Splatting" paper [S1]. It was developed to enhance training efficiency and reconstruction quality by carefully managing the introduction of appearance parameters and applying quantization techniques in the final stages of training. The method builds on the standard 3D Gaussian Splatting framework but introduces specific temporal controls for parameter optimization.
 
